 ],
 "literal": "Water flowing eyes-having-you, you here to stay behind, / who indeed are those capable of separating? Mountain slope's / ornament having, right-spiraling marāam tree's / summer beautiful branch fragrant, / sweetness-oozing bright forehead, even with you, the journey!",
 "translation": "With tears streaming from your eyes, if you were to stay behind here, who indeed could leave you? O one with a sweet-scented, bright forehead! His journey through the mountain slopes—adorned with the right-spiraling blossoms of the marāam tree that fragrance the summer from their beautiful branches—will surely be with you.",
 "modern": "கண்களில் நீர் வழிய நீ இங்கே தங்கிவிட, உன்னைப் பிரிந்து செல்பவர் யார்தான் இருக்கிறார்கள்? மலையின் பக்கத்தில், மலைக்கு அணிகலனாக விளங்கும், வலப்பக்கமாகச் சுழிந்த பூக்களைக் கொண்ட மராஅ மரத்தின் அழகிய கிளைகள் கோடைகாலத்தில் நறுமணம் வீசும். தேன் போன்ற நறுமணம் கமழும் ஒளிரும் நெற்றியை உடையவளே! அவனுடைய பயணம் உன்னோடும் சேர்ந்ததுதான்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the tōḻi (confidante) to comfort the talaivi (heroine) when the talaivaṉ (hero) plans a journey through the arid desert (pālai) to acquire wealth. The heroine is distressed at the prospect of separation, her eyes filled with tears. The confidante reassures her that no one could possibly have the heart to leave her behind in such grief. She points out that the mountain slopes are adorned with the beautiful, right-spiraling blossoms of the marāam tree, which spread a sweet fragrance even in the harsh summer. By comparing the heroine's sweet-scented, bright forehead to this beautiful landscape, the confidante implies that the hero's journey will not be a separation, but rather that he will take her along with him.",
